The  file  maps Ethernet MAC addresses to host names.  Lines con‐
sist of an address and a host name, separated by  any  number  of
blanks  and/or  tab characters.  A character indicates the begin‐
ning of a comment; characters up to the end of the line  are  not
interpreted  by routines which search the file.  Each line in has
the format: Ethernet MAC addresses are expressed as six hexadeci‐
mal numbers separated by colons, e.g.  "08:00:20:00:5a:bc".   The
functions described in and can read and produce this format.  The
traditional  use of involved using hostnames for the second argu‐
ment.  This may not be suitable for machines that  don’t  have  a
common MAC address for all interfaces (i.e., just about every non
machine).   There  should be no problem in using an IP address as
the second field if you wish to differentiate  between  different
interfaces  on a system.  The file resides in The file format was
adopted from and appeared in A name server should be used instead
of a static file.
